  "text": "umably, we want to find the sum of all positive integer divisors of $20!$ that are not divisible by $10$.\n\n폿\n폿\n\nשולט\n\n1. First, find all the divisors of $20!$.\n\n2. Then, filter out the divisors that are divisible by $10$.\n\n3. Finally, sum the remaining divisors and find the remainder when this sum is divided by $20!$.\n\nWhy쬬There's a chance we may encounter a large number because $20!$ is a large factorial and its divisors are products of primes up to $20$. Specifically:\n\n[*] $20! = 2^{18} \\times 3^9 \\times 5^4 \\times 7^3 \\times 11 \\times 13 \\times 17 \\times 19$\n[*] A divisor of $20!$ that is divisible by $10$ must have a factor of $2$ and at least one factor of $5$. Since we are focusing on divisors that are not divisible by $10$, we need to find those that do not include a factor of $2$ or only include a prime factor that does not include a factor of $2$ (i.e., it doesn't include a factor of $2$ or is not $2$).\n\n썻\n\nTo solve this problem:\n\n1. Generate all divisors of $20!$ (easily done by prime factorization).\n\n2. Exclude the divisors that are divisible by 10:\n\n    - Excluding the factors of 2 (i.e., any product of 2 cannot be divisors). This includes any power of 2 between 2^0 and 2^18.\n    - Excluding the divisors that are multiples of 10 (which includes products involving 2, 5, or multiples of 5 with non-multiple of 2).\n\n.Desc\n\n3. Sum up the remaining divisors.\n\n4. Use the \"modulo\" operation to find the remainder of this sum when divided by $20!$ (which is simply the sum because the sum of divisors of $20!$ is dividend by several factors of 2 and 5, which leaves the divisor of $20!$ unchanged as there are not enough prime factors of 5 nor 2 to balance out the 10-divisible factor in the sum).\n\nDesc",
